ions almost every day  there was something in there he said  that he found useful in fact he even  tells the story which I detail in the  book about how it helped avoid nuclear  Armageddon when India and Pakistan were  going to war with each other and  something he read in the PDB helped him  defuse that situation following that was  george w bush george w bush did what his  father had done take a briefing every  day with an intelligence brief  but he took the briefing process up two  notches one he decided from the  beginning he was gonna have a briefer  not only briefed him when he was in  washington but wherever he was in the  world and he had his CIA PDB briefer  travel with him on domestic and foreign  trips number two he also decided to  supplement the PDB briefing process in  his second term he added what were  called deep dives where in addition to  getting this daily document of  everything you needed to know he brought  in analysts the world-class experts  primarily from CIA and then from other  intelligence agencies as well to talk  with him in depth about the crises of  the day in the first 18 months after  they did this he had a had over two  hundred analysts come in to talk with  him in some cases for up to an hour in  depth about one particular country or  one particular issue that brings us to  our current President Barack Obama I  told you the PDB has been personalized  for every president that was made  obvious Monday when the White House  publicized a photo of the president  reading his president's Daily Brief on  an iPad the president's Daily Brief has  now electrons on a screen not ink on  paper still assessments of world affairs  but now presented in a way that can  allow multimedia graphics can allow  videos to be played things like that  it's easy to see this as a modern  invention because it's now on an iPad  but it's the same intelligence  assessments that started really with  Franklin Roosevelt back in World War two  when he was getting in